    cd = CategoryChartData()
    cd.categories = ['Q1 (lowest)', 'Q2', 'Q3', 'Q4 (highest)']
    cd.add_series('Implied elasticity', [r[0] for r in H[key]])
    ch = chart_frame(s, XL_CHART_TYPE.LINE_MARKERS, cd, x, 2.3, 5.8, 3.85)
    ser = ch.plots[0].series[0]
    no_line(ser)
    marker(ser, XL_MARKER_STYLE.CIRCLE, 12, INK if k == 0 else BRASS)
    add_errbars(ser, [r[1] for r in H[key]], color=INK if k == 0 else BRASS, w=1.5)
    for j, r in enumerate(H[key]):
        point_label(ser, j, f'{r[0]:.2f}', size=13, color=TEXT, pos=XL_LABEL_POSITION.RIGHT, b=True)
    style_axis(ch.value_axis, size=12, grid=True, line=False, numfmt='0')
    ch.value_axis.minimum_scale = -1; ch.value_axis.maximum_scale = 5; ch.value_axis.major_unit = 1
    style_axis(ch.category_axis, size=13, color=TEXT, line=True)
    ch.category_axis.tick_label_position = XL_TICK_LABEL_POSITION.LOW
add_text(s, LM, 6.2, CW, 0.6,
         ['Implied openness elasticity at quartile means from the interaction IV, 95% CI. Interacted first stages are weaker (KP F ≈ 6–10), so the gradient matters more than the point values. '
          'A reduced-form quintile check without weak-instrument issues gives the same shape (0.234 in the least-connected quintile, 0.016 in the most).'],
         size=11.5, color=MUTED, line=1.05)
notes(s, """
Who gains? We let the elasticity vary with a country's income and connectivity in 1996.
In both dimensions the implied openness elasticity falls from the lowest to the highest quartile: from about 1.5 to 0.7 across income, and from 2.4 to 1.5 across baseline connectivity.
The interacted first stages are weaker, with F between about 6 and 10, so the intervals are wide and we read this as a gradient rather than as precise numbers per quartile. A reduced-form check by quintile, which has no weak-instrument problem, gives the same shape.
The message: an additional air link is worth most in poorer and under-connected economies. Aviation can work as a convergence lever.
""")

# ======================================================================= 25 aggregate
