""")

# ======================================================================= 27 covid
s = content('03  Results  ·  Magnitudes', 'In reverse: connectivity fell in 154 of 175 countries in 2020')
s.shapes.add_picture(A('map_covid_2019_2020.png'), Inches(0.45), Inches(1.95), Inches(8.6))
add_text(s, 9.3, 2.0, 3.35, 0.3, [[('IMPLIED ONE-YEAR DISRUPTION', dict(spc=80, b=True))]], size=10.5, color=MUTED)
cv = [['Hub quality', [('$7.1 tn', dict(b=True, color=INK))]], ['Maximum hub', '$6.3 tn'], ['Total (sum)', '$1.7 tn']]
hairline_table(s, 9.3, 2.35, [1.9, 1.45], cv, row_h=0.45, size=15, header=0, align=[PP_ALIGN.LEFT, PP_ALIGN.RIGHT])
add_text(s, 9.3, 3.9, 3.35, 2.6, [
    dict(runs=['Largest implied losses: United States, Germany, Hong Kong, Korea, Japan.'], after=10),
    dict(runs=[('Read as a range. ', dict(b=True, color=INK)),
               'Long-run elasticities overstate short-run responses, and in 2020 connectivity and trade fell together under a common shock.'], after=0),
], size=14, color=TEXT, line=1.05)
footer(s, 'Hub quality measure; 2019 to 2020. Symmetry between expansion and collapse is an assumption of this exercise.')
notes(s, """
If the relationship is symmetric, a collapse should subtract trade. In 2020 hub quality fell in 154 of 175 countries.
Applying the same arithmetic implies a one-year disruption of about 7.1 trillion dollars on hub quality, 6.3 on the maximum hub and 1.7 trillion on the sum. The largest implied losses fall on the United States, Germany, Hong Kong, Korea and Japan.
We read these as a range and with two cautions. Long-run elasticities overstate short-run responses. And in 2020 connectivity and trade fell together under a common shock, the pandemic, so this is not the causal loss from connectivity alone.
""")

# ======================================================================= 28 conclusion
s = content('Conclusion', 'Air connectivity raises trade openness, through air-served goods, most where it is scarce')
finds = [
    ('1', 'A causal driver of integration', 'Openness elasticity 1.21, volume 2.13 (2SLS, KP F = 21.9).'),
    ('2', 'Works through composition', 'Trade tilts toward high value-to-weight and intermediate goods.'),
    ('3', 'Uneven returns', 'Largest in poorer and under-connected economies.'),
    ('4', 'Large in aggregate', 'About $10 tn of 2024 goods trade since 1996, in partial equilibrium.'),
]
yy = 2.05
for num, head, txt in finds:
    add_text(s, LM, yy - 0.1, 0.7, 0.8, [num], size=34, font=HEAD, color=BRASS)
    add_text(s, LM + 0.7, yy, 6.3, 0.4, [head], size=18, color=INK, b=True)
    add_text(s, LM + 0.7, yy + 0.4, 6.3, 0.5, [txt], size=15, color=TEXT)
    yy += 1.05
X3 = 8.15
add_text(s, X3, 2.0, 4.5, 0.3, [[('POLICY', dict(spc=100, b=True))]], size=10.5, color=BRASS)
add_text(s, X3, 2.3, 4.5, 1.2, ['Airport and route investment and air-service liberalisation are concrete trade levers, with the highest marginal returns in under-connected economies.'],
         size=15, color=TEXT, line=1.05)
hline(s, X3, 3.6, 12.63, 3.6, color=RULE)
add_text(s, X3, 3.75, 4.5, 0.3, [[('LIMITS', dict(spc=100, b=True))]], size=10.5, color=MUTED)
add_text(s, X3, 4.05, 4.5, 1.3, [dict(runs=t, bullet=True, after=3) for t in
                                  ['Partial-equilibrium aggregation', 'Identification from post-2010 variation', 'Weaker first stages in the heterogeneity models']],
         size=14, color=TEXT)
hline(s, X3, 5.2, 12.63, 5.2, color=RULE)
add_text(s, X3, 5.35, 4.5, 0.3, [[('NEXT', dict(spc=100, b=True))]], size=10.5, color=MUTED)
add_text(s, X3, 5.65, 4.5, 1.0, [dict(runs=t, bullet=True, after=3) for t in
                                  ['Bilateral flows in a gravity structure', 'General equilibrium with modal choice']],
         size=14, color=TEXT)
notes(s, """
To conclude. Air connectivity is a causal driver of trade integration, and it works on openness: an elasticity of 1.21, and 2.13 on volume.
It works through composition, tilting trade toward high value-to-weight and intermediate goods.
The returns are largest where connectivity is scarce, in poorer and under-connected economies.
And nearly three decades of connectivity growth account for on the order of 10 trillion dollars of 2024 goods trade, in partial equilibrium.
For policy, airport and route investment and air-service liberalisation are concrete trade levers, with the highest returns in under-connected economies.
The limits are the partial-equilibrium aggregation, identification from post-2010 variation, and weaker first stages in the heterogeneity models. Next we want bilateral flows in a gravity structure and a general-equilibrium model with modal choice. Thank you.
""")

# ======================================================================= 29 thanks
s = prs.slides.add_slide(BL)
bg(s, INK)
faint_map(s, 55)
add_text(s, 0.9, 2.3, 8, 1.2, ['Thank you'], size=54, font=HEAD, color=WHITE)
add_text(s, 0.9, 3.55, 7.5, 0.6, ['Global Air Connectivity and Trade Openness, 1996–2024'], size=19, color=ONDARK, i=True)
add_text(s, 0.9, 4.45, 8.2, 0.8, ['Sunbin YOO, Junya KUMAGAI, Longfei ZHENG, Chunan WANG, Jinwoo LEE, Anming ZHANG'], size=14, color=WHITE, line=1.1)
add_text(s, 0.9, 5.5, 7.0, 0.4, ['Comments and questions are very welcome.'], size=15, color=BRASS)
TITLES[len(prs.slides)] = 'Thank you'
notes(s, 'Thank you. I look forward to your comments and questions.')

prs.save(OUT)
json.dump(NOTES, open(os.path.join(HERE, 'notes.json'), 'w', encoding='utf-8'), ensure_ascii=False, indent=1)
print('saved', OUT, len(prs.slides), 'slides; notes words =', sum(len(t['notes'].split()) for t in NOTES))
